WEBVTT

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:02.820
It's that time again.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:10.660
Welcome to Tesla Short's Time Dally, episode 468, coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.240
It's May 10th, 2026.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.680
Let's get into what's moving in the Tesla world today.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.520
Tesla's drive to hire specialized Korean chip engineers in Seoul shows it's building its

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.520
own Silicon stack for future AI hardware rather than relying on outside suppliers.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:37.620
Tesla has posted openings for senior process engineers in lithography, dry etch, thin films,

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:43.180
and metrology to support Project Terra Fab with roles based in Seoul.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:50.800
The effort targets talent shaped by Samsung and SK hynics to bring wafer level fabrication

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.460
expertise in-house.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:59.400
This move points to Tesla's intent to control more of the semiconductor supply chain that

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.220
underpins its AI and autonomy hardware.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:08.700
By locating the effort in Seoul, Tesla gains access to a labor pool already trained on

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.780
sub-10 nanometer nodes, shortening the learning curve that would otherwise stretch years if

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.740
the company tried to build that expertise from scratch elsewhere matters for speed.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:26.540
If the hires translate into even partial in-house process capability, Tesla could shorten

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.740
iteration cycles between chip design and vehicle integration.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:37.500
That vertical step would matter most for autonomy hardware, where small improvements in power

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.900
or latency compound across millions of vehicles.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:47.040
After seeing how far Tesla is willing to go upstream on chips, it makes sense to look

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.660
at how the company is closing out one of its oldest vehicle lines.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.500
The last model S produced at Fremont carried signatures from the workers who built it.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.020
Production of the refreshed model S and Model X has now concluded at the plant.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:07.680
The milestone marks the end of an era for the original sedan line, while Fremont shifts

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.340
focus to newer programs.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:15.220
Tesla postponed the planned signature addition delivery event at Fremont without setting

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:15.780
a new date.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.360
The delay follows completion of the final model S and Model X units.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.320
Observers are watching whether the postponement signals a shift in how Tesla plans to mark

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.860
the end of current S and X production.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.740
While Fremont shifts focus, Tesla is still gaining ground in markets where it wants face resistance.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.820
Tesla moved ahead of Kia in South Korean EV registrations for the first time.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:49.600
The result reflects growing local acceptance of Tesla vehicles despite strong domestic competition.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:54.960
The shift could influence how other global EV makers approach the Korean market.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:02.240
Success in Korea stands in contrast to the tighter positioning challenges Tesla faces in its largest market.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:11.300
A detailed look at the Model YL variant examines how Tesla is positioning the vehicle against local rivals in China.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.940
The analysis weighs pricing, range, and feature differences that affect buyer choices.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:24.660
It highlights the trade-offs Tesla faces as competition intensifies in its largest market.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:32.580
On the hardware side, visibility for Tesla's other vehicles is also growing through unexpected voices.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:41.140
J. Leno drew an unexpected parallel between the Tesla semi and classic long haul trucks during a recent review.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:47.260
The comparison underscores how the electric truck handles weight and efficiency on real roads.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.420
It adds visibility for the semi as more operators begin evaluating it for fleet use.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.500
Kathy would place Tesla at the top of her list of AI and data center stocks.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:06.400
The ranking highlights expected gains from Robotaxi deployment as a key growth driver.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:14.080
Woods' long-term target reflects confidence in Tesla's ability to scale autonomy software across fleets.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:22.580
Tesla shared side-by-side images showing what a driver sees versus what the car's cameras and neural nets detect.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:29.700
The clips emphasize how the system tracks objects and road features beyond the visible light spectrum

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:37.320
in some cases. They serve as a reminder that perception improvements continue even as hardware stays fixed.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:42.980
A short post from Tesla stressed how fractions of a second affect crash outcomes.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:50.320
The message aligns with ongoing work on pre-collision systems that act before physical contact occurs.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.600
It underscores the value of fast sensor fusion and decision loops in the vehicle stack.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.400
Now one thing worth which is the open-ended delay around the signature addition event at Fremont.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:14.680
Tesla pushed back the signature addition delivery event at Fremont after the final model S and Model X units finished production.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:23.500
No new date has been announced, leaving owners and enthusiasts without a clear timeline for what was meant to be a milestone celebration.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:31.020
The open-ended delay risks dampening the sense of closure around the current S and X generation.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.900
You could prompt questions about whether production or logistics issues are still surfacing.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:52.320
Taking a step back from today's headlines, Tesla's decision to recruit wafer fabrication engineers directly in South Korea challenges the assumption that its vehicle business can keep scaling without deeper control over upstream silicon.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:58.820
The postings target process specialists in lithography, etch, and metrology.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:03.720
These are the exact disciplines that determine yield and cost at leading foundries.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:12.420
By locating the effort in Seoul, Tesla gains access to a labor pool already trained on sub-10 nanometer nodes.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:22.360
Shortening the learning curve that would otherwise stretch years if the company tried to build that expertise from scratch in the United States matters for timing.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:33.000
If the hires translate into even partial in-house process capability, Tesla could shorten iteration cycles between chip design and vehicle integration.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:44.080
That vertical step would matter most for autonomy hardware, where small improvements in power or latency compound across millions of vehicles.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:53.340
Keep an eye on any updates around the postponed signature edition event and how Tesla plans to mark the close of this production chapter.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.180
That's your Tesla news for today.

